WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:02.920
7,117.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.670
Okay, it would be a short video if I just
gave that one answer.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:11.080
But that’s the number according to
the 2020 edition of Ethnologue,

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.889
widely considered to be the closest thing
we have to a directory of languages.

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:19.250
But here’s the catch: languages are not
separate, distinct things.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.289
They evolve, split, merge, fall out of use.

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:25.599
And it's often difficult to say exactly where
one language ends, and another begins.

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:28.560
Sure, it's clear that my English and
another person's Mandarin

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.560
are completely different languages,

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.930
but what about my English
and someone else's Scots?

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.450
Or someone else's Singlish?

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.750
Or someone else’s rural
Southern American English,

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:41.880
which might not be understood
by the Dowager of Downton Abbey? 

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.980
And it’s not just about location, either.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.840
There’s a difference between
the English I use here,

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.970
versus what I actually use
when I'm talking to friends.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.160
And what about over time?

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.720
Where does English start to become
Middle English or Old English?

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.500
At some point, it’s different enough to
get a new label:

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.870
but there isn't a
naturally-occuring clean line.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:06.920
There’s an old saying that a language is
just a dialect with an army and a navy.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.430
Languages change to adapt to the needs
of their speakers.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.560
And there are a few reasons for that.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.030
Maybe it’s to provide new words for new
concepts.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:20.870
“Computer” used to be the term for someone
who was paid to sit and do calculations.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:23.830
Their job was to compute, they were a computer.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.190
When automated tools came along,
they took on the name.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.500
Sometimes languages change to
speed up communication.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.659
“Going to” wasn’t always a marker for
future tense:

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:37.720
and saying “I’m going to start” makes
no sense when taken literally.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.350
You can’t go to “start”,
start is not a place,

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.860
but the phrase “going to” became lexicalized.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.390
And then it turned into a word: “gonna”,

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.340
and then “I’m gonna” became “Imma”.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:53.690
That’s a word in common use that’s probably
under fifty years old. Still English.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.979
Sometimes a language changes because people
are trying to find an identity.

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:00.460
The words we use help us
fit in or to stand out.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.460
Language changes spread across populations,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.780
they act as markers of who you are
and who your group is.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:11.069
If enough people change their pronunciation, intonation,
their word and grammar choices, all together,

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:12.690
you end up with a dialect.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:16.110
If that happens over and over and over
for centuries, well,

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.390
you might end up with a new language.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.690
Or you might end up with an old one
falling out of use.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.990
Centuries of colonialism and globalization
have encouraged the death of local languages.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.530
And even fairly major ones can be in trouble.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.970
Icelandic is spoken by more than 300,000 people,

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.569
it’s the national language of
a European country.

00:02:34.569 --> 00:02:37.000
But it’s not supported by
iPhones or Android phones

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.930
If you speak Icelandic and you
want to use a smartphone,

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.370
so if you want to participate in modern life,

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.030
you need to speak another language,
probably English.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.030
Very little media is translated into Icelandic,

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:48.880
almost no-one who moves to Iceland
speaks it,

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.090
which means that Icelandic youth sometimes

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.410
just use English between each other
as a first language.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.090
Unless deliberate efforts are made
to keep it alive,

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.319
even a national language like Icelandic
could be at risk eventually.

00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:05.480
So, yes, Ethnologue counts 7,000 languages,

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.720
based on fairly reasonable lines
that they’ve decided on.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.790
Someone has to draw some lines somewhere,
so they do.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:17.740
But of those 7,000, 50% to 90% of them may be
functionally extinct by the end of the century.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.670
And while I’m sure there would be advantages
to one global language,

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.620
it’s not just about communication:

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.550
it’s about access to culture,
to history, to subtext,

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:30.280
to the insights that can come from one language
explaining in a single word

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.900
what would take another language fifty.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.010
If you’re watching this and thinking
“ah, it doesn’t matter too much”,

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.150
imagine how you’d feel if it was English
that was endangered,

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.349
and ask yourself if you’d be okay with the
next generations

00:03:42.349 --> 00:03:46.400
only having translations of the works
you grew up with.

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:48.670
If you want more linguistics,
then my co-author Gretchen McCulloch

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:49.670
has a podcast called Lingthusiasm.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.890
The link is in the description,
I recommend it.

